#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee48ef50-7901-5968-8c6e-3ce826fd0abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{3}y=\\frac{x}{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320868} {:answer/id #uuid "56a4decd-d69f-5134-a279-4ab7bfe6d830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320869} {:answer/id #uuid "b27cb28d-67df-56bb-b535-e57e5c99630d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320870} {:answer/id #uuid "3858e614-0306-5bff-bc1c-7bc4762692d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320871} {:answer/id #uuid "d0219253-1153-52cd-bc9a-45727be4ddec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3858e614-0306-5bff-bc1c-7bc4762692d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320871} :answer-field/id #uuid "23eb2ba6-4d1b-5c75-adbb-ad24e70a7072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16833ba9-454a-5aa5-8648-06e8c0ad9832" :question/math-academy-id "q-103551" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92148c48-e4d7-56ae-9599-2cc7bd96aa51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320875} {:answer/id #uuid "45dccf9a-1ae2-5822-ba0f-070623de48a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320876} {:answer/id #uuid "5bcadffa-4ca5-51f7-aafe-824a10127836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320877} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccac42c-b260-5c7a-bd80-38dd2ad4da7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320878} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9e0ce4-155c-553c-a397-bde95a14420e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45dccf9a-1ae2-5822-ba0f-070623de48a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320876} :answer-field/id #uuid "11ab446b-775b-5aed-ba93-70a89fadafed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6dcfb59a-7071-5f63-8843-aefb72bb7d96" :question/math-academy-id "q-331729" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=5$\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+7xy & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+7xy+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(7x+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94a38643-71ab-5649-8a0d-1f0a0bdbcd23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085392} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4f2660-fc99-5fe8-8355-d646203fcae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085393} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd15e50-101e-5283-bb7f-b784ef932453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085394} {:answer/id #uuid "faaf7af8-3a08-5003-a54e-672d648e3ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085395} {:answer/id #uuid "57051897-2625-5762-994d-3740964f0109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94a38643-71ab-5649-8a0d-1f0a0bdbcd23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085392} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6cc48f7-56e8-524f-9fd4-9b41ff89a516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7723c02-822f-5a83-b3af-429d938426d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-331744" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9cd1c63-69b1-52f3-8711-17938e086b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}={e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320882} {:answer/id #uuid "4369c4a4-f6ca-57b6-84d1-3e75fe079815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320883} {:answer/id #uuid "af635872-19ab-5485-a211-8a017451f378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320884} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8b18fa-229f-588f-b565-047923a9cb4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320885} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1bddd5-b02e-5e11-8930-0af82862fca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e1bddd5-b02e-5e11-8930-0af82862fca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320886} :answer-field/id #uuid "d81ca39c-dba9-54b9-8984-e3117520499a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae5b5cf7-dbe2-594e-9c2c-bc9d8807aa4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-79752" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\sqrt{y}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8787ecbb-bcde-5e1b-a5ed-9d23815522e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087109} {:answer/id #uuid "b54535fc-a8dc-5ada-b428-b490dbf1a18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087110} {:answer/id #uuid "c792fe58-82f5-5954-93e9-3119bea36ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087111} {:answer/id #uuid "6b67e556-e64d-523c-9321-f7fb1846ebc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087112} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9de150-76e4-5439-86c8-31f5b6d852a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = \\cos e^{\\arctan (x^{2} + \\ln x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c792fe58-82f5-5954-93e9-3119bea36ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087111} :answer-field/id #uuid "7759e328-b997-576f-b938-3880d1aeb387" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecc44493-c978-58e2-96eb-083d048c8f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8352" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}]}
